WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.440 --> 00:00:05.240
Hey folks, right up top—I have some opinions about&nbsp;
Monday’s bans, and those opinions obviously didn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.240 --> 00:00:10.080
fit into this video because it’s a precon review.&nbsp;
I made a separate video that went up on my Patreon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.080 --> 00:00:14.600
that gave some of my thoughts, and I also included&nbsp;
a portion of that video at the end of this one,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.600 --> 00:00:18.360
so you can stick around for that if you want&nbsp;
to know some of my thoughts. With that said,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.360 --> 00:00:21.920
let’s talk about the preconstructed commander deck&nbsp;
from Duskmourn that my patrons picked out for me&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.920 --> 00:00:26.760
to review—Endless Punishment. What I’m going to&nbsp;
do in this video is talk about what this deck&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.760 --> 00:00:31.520
is trying to do, what I like about the deck, and&nbsp;
what I’d change about the deck to improve it. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.520 --> 00:00:36.480
particular deck sees Wizards of the Coast delving&nbsp;
into an archetype relatively unexplored in precons&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.480 --> 00:00:42.480
of the past—group slug—and this particular deck&nbsp;
is pretty powerful showing. This begins with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.480 --> 00:00:49.760
commander—Valgavoth, Harrower of Souls is a 4 mana&nbsp;
4/4 flier in Rakdos colors with “Ward—Pay 2 life”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.760 --> 00:00:53.680
and “Whenever an opponent loses life for the&nbsp;
first time during each of their turns, put a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.680 --> 00:00:58.760
+1/+1 counter on Valgavoth and draw a card.” The&nbsp;
first thing I notice about this card is that it’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.760 --> 00:01:05.280
a strong payoff for a very specific action—causing&nbsp;
opponents to lose life on their own turns. There&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.280 --> 00:01:09.800
are all manner of vaguely sluggy cards that this&nbsp;
can work well with, but there’s one specific type&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.800 --> 00:01:14.520
of card that this really goes to town with, and&nbsp;
that’s cards that deal damage to opponents on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.520 --> 00:01:19.840
each of their turns. The prototypical example&nbsp;
in my mind is Sulfuric Vortex, but there are a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.840 --> 00:01:24.000
number of similar cards in Rakdos colors as well&nbsp;
such as Underworld Dreams type cards that deal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:28.960
damage to players when they draw cards. These go&nbsp;
so hard with Valgavoth because they allow a player&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.960 --> 00:01:33.920
to draw three additional cards per turn cycle, as&nbsp;
well as putting three +1/+1 counters on Valgavoth.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.800 --> 00:01:39.040
This will translate into a pretty back-breaking&nbsp;
amount of advantage, and since a lot of cards&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.040 --> 00:01:44.480
that do this with Valgavoth cost three mana, it’s&nbsp;
theoretically quite easy to set this up by turn 4.

00:01:44.480 --> 00:01:48.600
Going into this deck, I was kind of curious how&nbsp;
much of this sort of thing this precon would be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.600 --> 00:01:54.240
packing, and the answer is honestly more than I&nbsp;
expected. There are 11 different cards that I’d&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.240 --> 00:01:59.040
expect to trigger life loss more often than&nbsp;
not on an opponent’s turn, and an additional&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.040 --> 00:02:04.120
15 cards that will at least sometimes trigger&nbsp;
life loss on an opposing turn. Beyond these,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.120 --> 00:02:08.120
there are an additional 10 cards that will&nbsp;
cause some sort of life loss to opponents&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.120 --> 00:02:13.000
at sorcery speed, and it seems like this sort&nbsp;
of incremental chip damage is how this deck is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.000 --> 00:02:17.920
looking to close out the game. A small part of&nbsp;
this package seems to be a combination of cards&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.920 --> 00:02:22.880
that draw opponents additional cards and cards&nbsp;
that damage opponents when they draw. This is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.880 --> 00:02:28.080
backed up by a beefy removal suite that packs&nbsp;
answers for a wide range of threats. Finally,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.080 --> 00:02:33.160
a small package worth of lifegain is here to keep&nbsp;
the deck alive when the heat turns up. Overall,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.160 --> 00:02:37.080
this deck is looking to build advantage with its&nbsp;
commander while gradually knocking down opposing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.080 --> 00:02:42.160
life totals through a broad range of different&nbsp;
cards that can dish out incremental damage.

00:02:42.160 --> 00:02:47.440
So that’s the game plan, but how do I like what’s&nbsp;
going on here? Overall I like it quite a lot,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.440 --> 00:02:52.360
actually. I knew going in that with the potential&nbsp;
power of the deck’s commander, a lot of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.360 --> 00:02:57.880
quality of the deck was going to come down to how&nbsp;
far the deck leverages its commander synergies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.880 --> 00:03:02.720
and I’m not unhappy with where it’s at. As I&nbsp;
mentioned there are 11 cards that will trigger&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.720 --> 00:03:08.920
Valgavoth on most opposing turns, which is a&nbsp;
decent count. Archidekt’s built-in hypergeometric&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.920 --> 00:03:14.440
calculator tells me that this means a 57% chance&nbsp;
of having one of these in the deck’s opening hand,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.440 --> 00:03:19.640
and a 78% chance to find one of these in the&nbsp;
first five draws of the game. Given that all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.640 --> 00:03:24.560
of these except for Kaervek the Merciless cost&nbsp;
five or less mana, this means a high probability&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.560 --> 00:03:29.800
of being able to set up a hefty draw engine&nbsp;
by turn 5. I do note that the number of these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.800 --> 00:03:36.040
cards at three or less mana is only four, and&nbsp;
this makes a lot of sense—these cards combine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.040 --> 00:03:41.560
with the commander to produce a value engine that&nbsp;
will run away with games if unanswered, so I can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.560 --> 00:03:46.520
understand why Wizards of the Coast would want to&nbsp;
avoid a lot of the lower cost options. Though this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.520 --> 00:03:52.240
precon does include easily the most powerful of&nbsp;
these, Kederekt Parasite, which is unexpected but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.240 --> 00:03:57.480
cool—as a group slug enjoyer, I love to see this&nbsp;
powerful tech piece printed widely in a precon.

00:03:57.480 --> 00:04:01.680
Looking at the other 15 Valvagoth enablers,&nbsp;
there’s a wide variety of different effects&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.680 --> 00:04:06.720
here. Six of these are Blood Artist effects, which&nbsp;
make a decent bit of sense here—the deck packs a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.720 --> 00:04:11.920
good bit of removal and it also runs a relatively&nbsp;
high creature count of 32. The deck doesn’t have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.920 --> 00:04:16.360
much self-sacrifice, but I expect that it should&nbsp;
still manage to get triggers often enough for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.360 --> 00:04:20.640
these to feel useful. The rest are a mixture of&nbsp;
different things that will lead to occasional&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.640 --> 00:04:26.720
life loss on opposing turns. Overall, these have a&nbsp;
good bit of variety, and oddly I feel like that’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.720 --> 00:04:31.560
a good thing with a commander this potent. A lot&nbsp;
of players building Valgavoth are going to default&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.560 --> 00:04:37.320
to cramming as many every-turn-pings as possible&nbsp;
into one deck, and though this does maximize the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.320 --> 00:04:41.760
possible Valgavoth triggers, it also flattens&nbsp;
the play pattern of the deck into one that is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.760 --> 00:04:47.200
predictably brutal for opponents. This will&nbsp;
lead to the deck being enemy #1 at all times&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.200 --> 00:04:52.000
and not having many cards dedicated to the backup&nbsp;
plans that will inevitably be necessary against&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.000 --> 00:04:57.160
competent opponents. By contrast, having a more&nbsp;
diverse range of Valgavoth enablers means that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.160 --> 00:05:01.840
this deck’s play patterns and relationship with&nbsp;
its commander are more textured to opponents,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.840 --> 00:05:06.480
and a lot of these enablers can also serve&nbsp;
useful purposes outside of enabling Valgavoth.

00:05:06.480 --> 00:05:10.840
So, the instant speed ping plan looks pretty good&nbsp;
to me. As I mentioned earlier, this deck’s removal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.840 --> 00:05:16.120
suite is likewise pretty good—it has seven pieces&nbsp;
of targeted removal, four board wipes, three&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.120 --> 00:05:21.560
removal engines, and one Kardur. He’s not exactly&nbsp;
removal, but he removes some annoying scary things&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.560 --> 00:05:25.680
from my presence by sending them at a different&nbsp;
player for a turn, and that’s basically the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.680 --> 00:05:30.040
same thing, right? There’s also Brash Taunter, I&nbsp;
guess that’s a removal engine if your opponents’&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.040 --> 00:05:35.320
creatures are puny. Beyond removal, the deck also&nbsp;
has moderate amounts of ramp and non-commander&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.320 --> 00:05:39.960
draw. This is a deck that’s gonna be drawing a&nbsp;
lot of cards and wanting to pay a lot of commander&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.960 --> 00:05:45.320
tax, so having a healthy collection of mana rocks&nbsp;
is absolutely a good thing. Non-commander draw is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.320 --> 00:05:50.680
also good—expecting to have the commander in play&nbsp;
at all times is highly optimistic, and fitting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.680 --> 00:05:55.240
in a healthy share of other draw is also good.&nbsp;
Finally, I support the choice to have some amount&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.240 --> 00:05:59.640
of life total stabilization here as well, which&nbsp;
includes a few different sources of life gain.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.320 --> 00:06:04.680
This is a deck that’ll attract attacks, and&nbsp;
it’s good to be able to weather a bit of that.

00:06:04.680 --> 00:06:09.760
Finally, the mana base here looks quite solid,&nbsp;
if a little tap land oriented. In the lands alone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.760 --> 00:06:15.160
we see 25 symbols each for red and black, which&nbsp;
are fantastic counts. There’s a lovely selection&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.160 --> 00:06:19.600
of different dual lands—a tango, a cycler, a&nbsp;
checkland, a pain land, a fast land, a reveal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.600 --> 00:06:25.240
land, an Odyssey filter, and most exciting of all,&nbsp;
a Graven Cairns, Future Sight art. This is great&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.240 --> 00:06:30.240
variety in lands for a two color precon. As far as&nbsp;
utility lands go, the deck runs Leechridden Swamp&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.240 --> 00:06:35.400
and Shivan Gorge to go with Valgavoth, a Witch’s&nbsp;
Clinic to grant lifelink, and Spinerock Knoll as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.400 --> 00:06:39.560
a hideaway land. There are some minor tweaks&nbsp;
that could be made to tune up this mana base,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.560 --> 00:06:43.600
and I’ll be doing one of them in my upgrade&nbsp;
on this deck, but honestly this manabase is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:43.600 --> 00:06:48.560
going to play just fine right out of the box. I&nbsp;
playtested the deck 20 times to get a feel for it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.560 --> 00:06:53.160
and in the vast majority of these the deck felt&nbsp;
pretty great, curving out relatively smoothly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.160 --> 00:06:56.520
setting up threats and engines pretty&nbsp;
consistently, and seeming generally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.520 --> 00:07:01.640
well-poised to push later into the game. In some&nbsp;
ways I find this deck tricky to evaluate because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.640 --> 00:07:06.640
it seems like a strong deck, but also a deck that&nbsp;
will be a lightning rod for opposing removal and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.640 --> 00:07:12.280
attacks. However, keeping that in mind gives us&nbsp;
a good idea of where to start with upgrading,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.280 --> 00:07:16.426
so with that said let’s get into the&nbsp;
things I’d do to try and improve this deck.

00:07:16.426 --> 00:07:16.517
For the rest of the video I’m going to discuss&nbsp;
parts of the deck I don’t like as much,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.517 --> 00:07:19.280
and changes I’d make to fix those parts. In&nbsp;
previous precon reviews I’ve subbed out 20 cards,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.280 --> 00:07:24.400
but in this one I’m going to drop that down&nbsp;
to 15—this base is just really solid and 20&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.400 --> 00:07:28.600
would feel like changing the deck much more than&nbsp;
needed. With that in mind, let’s get into some of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.600 --> 00:07:34.240
the cards I’m not as thrilled by in this, starting&nbsp;
with basically a whole category of card—Sorcery&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.240 --> 00:07:39.960
speed slug cards. These are cards that deal damage&nbsp;
to opponents, but usually or always on our turn&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:39.960 --> 00:07:44.560
rather than our opponents’ turns, and which thus&nbsp;
can’t trigger Valgavoth. These cards do have a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.560 --> 00:07:48.560
valid place in the deck—obviously Valgavoth&nbsp;
won’t always be in play, and cards that slug&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.560 --> 00:07:53.040
opponents on our turn might be better than their&nbsp;
instant-speed counterparts sometimes. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.040 --> 00:07:58.360
I do hold these to a higher bar than instant-speed&nbsp;
options, and that is a bar that simply is simply&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.360 --> 00:08:03.280
not met by most of these. Enchanter’s Bane&nbsp;
is theoretically an enchantment hate option,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.280 --> 00:08:08.440
but in practice it seems undertuned and gives&nbsp;
opponents too many ways around being harmed by it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.440 --> 00:08:12.840
Fear of Burning Alive is a cute potential damage&nbsp;
amplifier, but this deck doesn’t have enough ways&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.840 --> 00:08:18.720
to enable Delirium, and without Delirium it’s&nbsp;
basically just a 6 mana 4/4. Combustible Gearhulk&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.720 --> 00:08:23.920
is a likewise fun card, and I love it in my Radha&nbsp;
deck whose average mana value is stupid high, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:23.920 --> 00:08:28.680
it too costs a lot of mana and this deck doesn’t&nbsp;
want the draw enough to warrant its inclusion.

00:08:28.680 --> 00:08:33.280
Speaking of draw, Stormfist Crusader feeds the&nbsp;
whole table cards, which might be okay in a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.280 --> 00:08:37.200
draw hungry deck, but this deck has powerful&nbsp;
enough draw in the command zone that giving&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:37.200 --> 00:08:42.600
opponents resources isn’t worth it. I exclude&nbsp;
Spiteful Visions for similar reasons—that one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:42.600 --> 00:08:47.160
actually is a potent Valgavoth enabler, but&nbsp;
in a group slug game plan you don’t want to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.160 --> 00:08:50.800
be giving your opponents resources to fight&nbsp;
you with, because you will be attracting the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:50.800 --> 00:08:55.840
ire of the whole table. There’s a reason why my&nbsp;
Rakdos group slug deck does exactly the opposite&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:55.840 --> 00:09:00.400
of giving opponents cards. If you think you can&nbsp;
bribe opponents to be nice to you with cards,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:00.400 --> 00:09:03.720
I think you’re underestimating how much your&nbsp;
opponents hate seeing their life total spiral&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:03.720 --> 00:09:09.480
downward, especially if it’s in tandem with you&nbsp;
drawing cards. A number of other cards I cut&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:09.480 --> 00:09:14.440
because they don’t do quite enough to warrant&nbsp;
keeping around. Vial Smasher’s ability doesn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:14.440 --> 00:09:19.000
deal enough damage to make up for its otherwise&nbsp;
meager body, Star Athlete commits the cardinal sin&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:19.000 --> 00:09:24.080
of giving opponents choices, and Tectonic Giant&nbsp;
is fine but a bit underwhelming here for four&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:24.080 --> 00:09:29.440
mana. Grab the Prize and Light up the Stage are&nbsp;
fine as filtering and draw options, respectively,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:29.440 --> 00:09:33.720
but decidedly unimpressive to me compared&nbsp;
to some other options in this sphere. Some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:33.720 --> 00:09:38.480
other cards are strong but feel out of place&nbsp;
in this deck—Braids is a sweet value engine,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:38.480 --> 00:09:42.680
but this deck doesn’t run many tokens or ETB&nbsp;
permanents that would be desirable sacrifice&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:42.680 --> 00:09:47.800
options. Kaervek can be nice if he sticks around,&nbsp;
but 7 mana is a bit too much to pay for what he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:47.800 --> 00:09:53.400
does for this deck. Finally, Barbflare Gremlin&nbsp;
is a card with potential, but this deck isn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:53.400 --> 00:09:58.200
set up well to break parity on the mana boost it&nbsp;
gives. Pro tip: doubling your opponents’ mana is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:58.200 --> 00:10:03.240
generally not recommended—Treasonous Ogre is a&nbsp;
reasonably popular card, and our gremlin friend&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:03.240 --> 00:10:08.400
here gives a three times better rate for our&nbsp;
opponents to translate life for additional mana.

00:10:08.400 --> 00:10:13.000
What goes in in place of these cards?&nbsp;
Well, most of my additions follow one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:13.000 --> 00:10:18.680
core principle—this deck will make opponents&nbsp;
square the hell up. One form of this is that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:18.680 --> 00:10:23.920
the commander easily forms many disgusting value&nbsp;
engines, and as a result will be a high priority&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:23.920 --> 00:10:28.520
target for removal. One way I choose to&nbsp;
combat this is protection equipments,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:28.520 --> 00:10:32.960
specifically Swiftfoot Boots and Champion’s&nbsp;
Helm. The other way I choose to combat this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:32.960 --> 00:10:38.920
is Glimpse the Impossible which works as a&nbsp;
potential burst of mana, as well as serving&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:38.920 --> 00:10:44.320
extra purposes of draw and blockers. I actually&nbsp;
dislike a couple of the cards I kept in the deck,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:44.320 --> 00:10:49.960
namely Rakdos Lord of Riots and Seance Board,&nbsp;
but these were kept because I expect this deck&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:49.960 --> 00:10:56.400
to do a lot of recasting its commander and these&nbsp;
cards are undeniably helpful for that. Moving on,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:56.400 --> 00:11:01.760
I mentioned squaring up earlier, and aside from&nbsp;
removal the way I expect this to manifest is this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:01.760 --> 00:11:07.320
deck getting attacked heavily. Opponents do not&nbsp;
like feeling like they’re on a clock, and cards&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:07.320 --> 00:11:12.560
that do consistent player damage over time will&nbsp;
trigger exactly that feeling. To mitigate that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:12.560 --> 00:11:18.240
I’m choosing to add some defensive cards to the&nbsp;
deck, namely goad and lifegain. Goad also has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:18.240 --> 00:11:22.600
the side benefit of enabling the deck’s Blood&nbsp;
Artist effects, since opponents being in combat&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:22.600 --> 00:11:27.560
with each other is a thing that often results in&nbsp;
creatures dying at no cost to the goading player.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:27.560 --> 00:11:33.360
Vengeful Ancestor is a classic goad-granter that&nbsp;
also triggers Valgavoth, and Frenzied Gorespawn&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:33.360 --> 00:11:39.080
is another goad ETB creature with some added&nbsp;
utility. For noncreature options, Disrupt Decorum&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:39.080 --> 00:11:44.480
is that lovely effect of goading everything, and&nbsp;
Spectacular Showdown is a similar card that can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:44.480 --> 00:11:50.200
also be used to suddenly kill an opponent with&nbsp;
an enlarged Valgavoth. For lifegain, Raphael,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:50.200 --> 00:11:56.320
Fiendish Savior is a lovely package—lifelink for&nbsp;
the commander and blockers in the form of devils,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:56.320 --> 00:12:00.880
and these devils can even enable Valgavoth&nbsp;
after they block. I’m also choosing to add&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:00.880 --> 00:12:06.320
Tainted Bonds, which serves double duty as&nbsp;
life total stabilizer and Valgavoth enabler.

00:12:06.320 --> 00:12:11.080
With defense and protection out of the way, let’s&nbsp;
talk about other Valgavoth enablers. I mentioned&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:11.080 --> 00:12:15.920
earlier that a common choice for naive players&nbsp;
building Valgavoth will be cramming in as many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:15.920 --> 00:12:20.960
cards as possible that deal damage to opponents&nbsp;
on each turn. While I do think that going all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:20.960 --> 00:12:25.600
in these would amplify the deck’s weaknesses&nbsp;
to removal and opposing aggression, adding in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:25.600 --> 00:12:29.640
a handful of these to improve consistency still&nbsp;
seems useful now that we’ve contained the deck’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:29.640 --> 00:12:34.440
weaknesses a bit. And I do mean a handful—as&nbsp;
I mentioned earlier this deck is already doing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:34.440 --> 00:12:40.040
pretty decently for Valgavoth enablers, and I just&nbsp;
want to add in a few more low-cost ones. Roiling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:40.040 --> 00:12:45.160
Vortex is a good enabler for Valgavoth that&nbsp;
damages players each turn, and I chose this over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:45.160 --> 00:12:50.280
Sulfuric Vortex because this deck has a decent&nbsp;
bit of lifegain that it’d be bad to incidentally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:50.280 --> 00:12:55.880
prevent. I’m also adding one of my pet cards—Rug&nbsp;
of Smothering—which performs the role of enabling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:55.880 --> 00:13:01.000
Valgavoth when opponents cast spells, along with&nbsp;
the similar Magebane Lizard. For a handful of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:01.000 --> 00:13:05.840
miscellaneous cards to finish things off, I added&nbsp;
the MDFC Hagra Mauling to replace Temple of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:05.840 --> 00:13:11.960
False God, and I also added another removal spell&nbsp;
I really like in slug decks called Cut. It’s a mid&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:11.960 --> 00:13:16.600
removal spell that can be cast from the graveyard&nbsp;
as a decent life loss spell, and I’ve found this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:16.600 --> 00:13:21.400
combo to be quite sweet—you get cheap removal for&nbsp;
something when you need it early and then a burst&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:21.400 --> 00:13:25.720
of life loss later when you’re getting to the&nbsp;
stage of finishing off opponents. My final add&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:25.720 --> 00:13:31.800
was a card I just like—Caustic Bronco. It’s a&nbsp;
draw engine, it can slug—what’s not to like?

00:13:31.800 --> 00:13:36.280
So that’s my upgrade—improved enablers, more&nbsp;
protection, and some defensive options to keep&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:36.280 --> 00:13:41.040
us alive through some irritated opponents. Even&nbsp;
without upgrades this is an impressive deck,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:41.040 --> 00:13:46.160
and it’s a deck that very clearly positions itself&nbsp;
as a table enemy. I like this about it—though the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:46.160 --> 00:13:50.440
commander has synergies that very much demand&nbsp;
removal immediately, which is an ever-common&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:50.440 --> 00:13:54.560
commander dynamic I don’t care for as much, the&nbsp;
fact that this is paired with a gameplan that is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:54.560 --> 00:13:59.280
looking to whittle down life totals quickly&nbsp;
is pretty fun. I’m also interested to see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:59.280 --> 00:14:03.040
if Wizards of the Coast diversifies the&nbsp;
gameplans of precons—there have been a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:03.040 --> 00:14:07.960
few different aggressive ones over the past&nbsp;
year, and I hope we see more of that. Alright,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:07.960 --> 00:14:11.840
that’s all I have for this video. If you want&nbsp;
to hear my thoughts on Duskmourn as a set,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:11.840 --> 00:14:15.960
I was on a podcast with The Trinket Mage and&nbsp;
3/3 Elk last week, and a link to that is in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:15.960 --> 00:14:21.280
the description. With that said, here are some of&nbsp;
my thoughts on yesterday’s [Monday's] bannings.

